Generate a per-request id (uuid or similar), attach it to `req` and to every log line, and echo it back as a response header. This matters more than usual here because of the `write()` failure-masking issue we just walked through — if a request hangs or the process crashes from an unhandled rejection, a request id lets you match "this request started at T" in the access log against whatever partial DB-side logging exists, even though the HTTP layer itself never got a response to log.

**5. The gap logging alone won't fix**
Global request logging only sees what reaches the HTTP response. It won't capture the original error in `db.write()`'s catch block (db.js:20-22), which — as discussed — gets clobbered by a failing `ROLLBACK` before it ever propagates up. If you want the *actual* DB error on record, that needs its own `logger.error(err)` (or similar) inside `write()`'s catch block, logged before attempting rollback, not just a generic access-log line at the Express layer. Otherwise "connection dropped mid-write" shows up in logs only as a mysterious hung/failed request with no root cause.

