This is a broken object-level authorization / IDOR bug: three handlers key their `UPDATE` solely off `req.params.id`, never checking that the row belongs to the caller's account. Meanwhile `req.user.accountId` (set by the `sessionUser` middleware in `auth.js`, mounted globally in `app.js`) is available in every handler and already used correctly elsewhere — `orders.js:6-12` binds `req.user.accountId` on insert. The three broken spots:

**`routes/orders.js:14-20`** — `PATCH /orders/:id/cancel` doesn't check the order belongs to the caller's account:
```js
// current
const row = await db.write(
  "UPDATE orders SET status = 'cancelled' WHERE id = $1 RETURNING *",
  [req.params.id]
);

// fixed
const row = await db.write(
  "UPDATE orders SET status = 'cancelled' WHERE id = $1 AND account_id = $2 RETURNING *",
  [req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
);
if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
```
